<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qufu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qufu is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, the capital of the State of Lu in the Zhou period and the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>. Its Temple of Confucius, the Kong family mansion and the Cemetery of Confucius form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The descendants of Confucius lived there for more than two thousand years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qufu.jpg" alt="Qufu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 호고호 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhou_Gong_Miao_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cult of Confucius grew: Han emperors offered sacrifices at his home town of <b>Qufu</b>, where his family temple became a vast complex, and from the Tang dynasty a temple to him stood beside the school of every prefecture and county.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital <b>Qufu</b>, in south-western Shandong, was built on land associated with the earlier Shang ally Yan, and Lu was charged with keeping order among the eastern peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-lu">State of Lu</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhu was ruled by the Cao clan, said to descend from the legendary Zhuanxu, and was recognised by the Zhou as a minor state in south-western Shandong, just south of <b>Qufu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-zou">State of Zou</a></li></ul></section>
